DHCP — How It Works

DHCP = Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol

DHCP automatically gives a device its network configuration, such as:

IP address
Subnet mask
Default gateway
DNS server
Lease time

For example, when you connect your laptop to Wi-Fi:

Laptop
   |
   | "I need an IP address"
   ↓
Wi-Fi Router / DHCP Server
   |
   | "Here is 192.168.1.20"
   ↓
Laptop

So you don't normally have to manually configure an IP address.

Where does DHCP come from?

There are two things to separate:

1. DHCP is a protocol

DHCP defines how the client and server communicate to obtain network configuration.

2. DHCP Server is software

A DHCP server program runs on some device.

For example:

Home network:

Laptop ──┐
Phone  ──┼── Wi-Fi Router
TV     ──┘       |
                 |
          DHCP Server software

Most home routers have DHCP server functionality built into them.

In a company, DHCP may run on a dedicated server.

For example:

Employees' PCs
      |
      v
    Switch
      |
      v
DHCP Server

On Linux, DHCP server software can be installed and configured on a Linux server.

In cloud environments, DHCP can be provided as part of the cloud networking infrastructure rather than you manually installing a DHCP server.

How DHCP Works — DORA

DHCP commonly works through 4 steps, remembered as:

DORA = Discover → Offer → Request → Acknowledge

Suppose your laptop has just connected to Wi-Fi.

Step 1: DHCP Discover

Your laptop doesn't have an IP address yet.

It broadcasts:

Laptop
   |
   | DHCP DISCOVER
   | "Is there any DHCP server?"
   ↓
Network

The client essentially says:

"I need an IP address."

Step 2: DHCP Offer

The DHCP server receives the request and offers an IP address.

For example:

DHCP Server
     |
     | DHCP OFFER
     | IP: 192.168.1.20
     | Mask: 255.255.255.0
     | Gateway: 192.168.1.1
     | DNS: 8.8.8.8
     ↓
Laptop

The server is saying:

"You can use 192.168.1.20."

Step 3: DHCP Request

The laptop responds:

Laptop
   |
   | DHCP REQUEST
   | "I want 192.168.1.20"
   ↓
DHCP Server
Step 4: DHCP ACK

The DHCP server confirms:

DHCP Server
     |
     | DHCP ACK
     | "Okay, you can use it."
     ↓
Laptop

Now the laptop has its network configuration.

Complete Flow
              DHCP Server
                   |
                   |
Laptop             |
  |                |
  | DHCP DISCOVER   |
  |--------------->|
  |                |
  | DHCP OFFER     |
  |<---------------|
  |                |
  | DHCP REQUEST   |
  |--------------->|
  |                |
  | DHCP ACK       |
  |<---------------|
  |
  ↓
IP = 192.168.1.20
What exactly does DHCP give?

DHCP doesn't only give an IP address.

For example, the DHCP server might provide:

IP Address       : 192.168.1.20
Subnet Mask      : 255.255.255.0
Default Gateway  : 192.168.1.1
DNS Server       : 8.8.8.8
Lease Time       : 24 hours

Your computer needs these settings to communicate properly.

If you're asking “How does the first IP, 192.168.1.1, get there?”, especially the Default Gateway, here's the key idea.

192.168.1.1 is usually the router's IP

It is not the first IP given by DHCP.

Your home router has a network interface with an IP configured on it, for example:

Router
LAN IP = 192.168.1.1
       |
       | DHCP Server
       |
       ├── Laptop → 192.168.1.10
       ├── Phone  → 192.168.1.11
       └── TV     → 192.168.1.12

The router's LAN IP (192.168.1.1) is configured when the router is set up/manufactured. The router then runs a DHCP server and gives other devices addresses from its configured pool.

For example:

DHCP pool:
192.168.1.10 → 192.168.1.254

So when your laptop connects:

Laptop
   ↓
DHCP Discover
   ↓
Router (192.168.1.1)
   ↓
DHCP Offer
   ↓
192.168.1.10

The laptop may receive:

IP Address      : 192.168.1.10
Subnet Mask     : 255.255.255.0
Default Gateway : 192.168.1.1
DNS Server      : 192.168.1.1
But why 192.168.1.1 specifically?

There is nothing special about .1.

The router could instead use:

192.168.0.1
192.168.1.1
192.168.10.1
10.0.0.1

It depends on how the network is configured.

