Devices in a Network

Network devices are hardware devices that connect computers, control traffic, or provide network services.

1. Router

A router connects different networks and forwards packets between them.

Example:

Home Network
192.168.1.0/24
       |
       v
    Router
       |
       v
   Internet

Main job: Routing based on IP addresses.



Forwards data	Specific destination port	All ports
Layer	Layer 2	Layer 1
Hub = "Send to everyone"

Switch = "Send to the correct device"

2. Switch

A switch connects devices within the same LAN.

PC ──┐
PC ──┤
PC ──┼── Switch
PC ──┘

It mainly uses MAC addresses to forward Ethernet frames.

Main job: Connect devices in a LAN.

3. Hub

A hub also connects multiple devices, but it sends incoming data to all ports.

PC ──┐
PC ──┤
PC ──┼── Hub
PC ──┘

It is an older and less efficient device than a switch.


4. Modem

Modem = Modulator-Demodulator

It connects your network to the service provider's network.

Home Network
     |
   Router
     |
   Modem
     |
 ISP / Internet

Modern home devices often combine modem + router + Wi-Fi + switch in one box.

7. Firewall

A firewall controls network traffic based on security rules.

For example:

Internet
   |
   v
Firewall
   |
   v
Internal Network

It can allow or block traffic based on things such as:

Source IP
Destination IP
Port
Protocol
Security rules


5. Access Point (AP)

An Access Point provides wireless connectivity to devices.

Laptop ))))
Phone  )))) → Access Point → Network
Tablet ))))

It allows Wi-Fi devices to join a wired or wireless network.
It means an Access Point (AP) is a device that allows wireless devices to connect to a network using Wi-Fi.

Simple example
Laptop  ))))
Phone   ))))  →  Access Point  →  Switch  →  Router  →  Internet
Tablet  ))))

Here:

)))) = Wi-Fi/wireless connection
Access Point = provides the Wi-Fi connection
Switch = connects network devices
Router = connects your local network to other networks/Internet
Real-life example

In an office, you may have:

Internet
   ↓
Router
   ↓
Switch
   ↓
Access Point
   ↓
Wi-Fi
 ↓   ↓   ↓
Laptop Phone Tablet

So, AP = a device that converts a wired network connection into Wi-Fi connectivity for wireless devices.

AP vs Router

Don't confuse them:

Access Point → mainly provides Wi-Fi access
Router → connects different networks and routes IP packets

A home Wi-Fi router usually has an Access Point built into it, which is why you can connect your phone directly to your home router using Wi-Fi.

Router → Internet

The router performs NAT (Network Address Translation) and changes the private source address to the router's public IP.
Thing	Role
Default Gateway	Tells your laptop where to send traffic outside its network
Router	Forwards traffic between networks
NAT	Translates private IP ↔ public IP
Public IP	Identifies your network to the Internet

Your laptop has this configuration

Suppose:

Laptop IP       = 192.168.1.10
Default Gateway = 192.168.1.1

And your router has:

Router LAN IP   = 192.168.1.1

So notice:

Default Gateway = 192.168.1.1
Router LAN IP   = 192.168.1.1

They are the same IP address in this example.

But they describe different things:
Router = the actual device
Default Gateway = the address your laptop uses to reach that device when going outside its local network
A router is a device that forwards packets between networks, while a default gateway is the IP address/interface that a host uses to send traffic to destinations outside its local network.
